2026-09-26 — TODO K11e; classification, not compiler repair or original PASS.

The direct upstream syscall_errors_test assumes no prior rseq registration.
The frozen glibc registers the main thread before main. kernel/rseq.c:502-512
rejects a different registration address with EINVAL before access_ok at
532-533 can return EFAULT. The original invalid address is aligned -4096UL.
The upstream companion run_syscall_errors_test.sh sets glibc.pthread.rseq=0
before exec. Both are separate programs in the unchanged 970-test population.

Small reference-built C probe, both retained kernels:
  default glibc registration, invalid address -> EINVAL
  disable registration before the probe's exec, same address -> EFAULT
  register private aligned 32-byte area -> success
  invalid different address -> EINVAL
  duplicate own registration -> EBUSY
  unregister own area -> success; invalid address -> EFAULT again
Two TAP assertions PASS on each kernel, zero FAIL/SKIP, clean shutdown.
The probe does not unregister glibc's area or impose a tunable on other tests.

Fresh unchanged originals through their existing complete runtime/wrapper:
  GCC: direct FAIL, companion script PASS
  EvoCC: direct FAIL, companion script PASS
All four boot/shutdown predicates PASS. Upstream timeout=0 remains untouched;
diagnostic whole-boot bound 105 seconds, all cases finish normally. No test
bytes, assertions, argv or wrapper policy changed. Direct FAIL remains FAIL.
This is a reproducible libc/test-precondition mismatch, not source UB or an
EvoCC miscompile. No historical full-run numerator changes and K13 remains open.

Build: GCC14 in evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none, one CPU/2 GiB:
gcc-14 -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror rseq-registration-order.c -o rseq-registration-order
